#%PAM-1.0
# Tilo greetd PAM stack — used by greetd (and therefore the graphical
# tilo-greeter) to authenticate the user before StartSession.
#
# Fingerprint is offered first as a *sufficient* auth factor: if
# pam_fprintd.so authenticates the user, the auth phase short-circuits to
# success; otherwise PAM falls through to the password substack. The
# leading "-" makes the line a silent no-op when the module is not present
# in the image (i.e. when the `fprintd-pam` package is absent), so this
# file is safe to ship whether or not fingerprint support is installed.
#
# fprintd drives the conversation by emitting PAM_TEXT_INFO / PAM_ERROR_MSG
# prompts ("Place your finger on the reader", "swipe didn't match, try
# again"); greetd relays those as AuthMessage{Info,Error}, which the
# greeter's conversation loop displays and continues past — the
# authoritative result is the top-level greetd Success/Error.
#
# Everything else defers to the distro's maintained `password-auth` stack
# (pam_unix for auth/account/password, and pam_systemd/limits/keyinit for
# the session), which keeps the always-present, VM-testable password login
# working and registers the started session with logind.
#
# Tilo stores an existing identity's private signing share in the user's
# freedesktop Secret Service.  Capture the successful password and start /
# unlock gnome-keyring in the PAM session exactly as GDM does.  Without these
# two hooks a direct greetd login authenticates successfully but the desktop's
# first identity lookup receives ENOKEY and falsely presents recovery chrome.
# The Tilo image installs gnome-keyring-pam and checks that contract below.
# systemd-homed: per-user encrypted home (LUKS-backed), unlocked at login and
# locked at logout/suspend. For a homed user pam_systemd_home authenticates +
# activates the home (sufficient = short-circuit success); for a traditional
# user it returns ignore and PAM falls through to the stack below — so this is a
# no-op for the default account. The leading "-" tolerates the module's absence.
-auth      sufficient   pam_fprintd.so
-auth      sufficient   pam_systemd_home.so
auth       substack     password-auth
# After the password is verified: pam_gnome_keyring's auth hook only captures
# the authtok an earlier module obtained (it never prompts), so placed before
# password-auth it saw no password and left the login keyring locked, and the
# first libsecret client (Chromium) made gcr-prompter ask for it.  Fedora's
# gdm-password stack has the same order.
auth       optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so

-account   sufficient   pam_systemd_home.so
account    include      password-auth

-password  sufficient   pam_systemd_home.so
password   substack     password-auth

# The session leaves greetd's own domain (xdm_t) for the user's SELinux
# context and gets its login uid, as /etc/pam.d/login does. Without these
# the compositor and every app it starts run as the display manager, whose
# policy forbids what a user session needs (scopes, sandboxes, home access).
session    required     pam_selinux.so close
session    required     pam_loginuid.so
session    required     pam_selinux.so open
session    required     pam_namespace.so
session    optional     pam_keyinit.so force revoke
-session   optional     pam_systemd_home.so
session    include      password-auth
session    optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start
